<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radhanites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Radanites, Radhanite, Radanim, ar-Raðaniyya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Radhanites were early medieval Jewish merchants active in the trade between Christendom and the Muslim world from roughly the 8th to the 10th centuries. They are known chiefly from the Book of Roads and Kingdoms of Ibn Khordadbeh, written in about 870, which describes them as multilingual and traces four routes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. They carried goods of small bulk and high demand, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, perfumes, jewellery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, swords and slaves. Whether the name meant a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, a clan or Jewish merchants in general is unclear. Their activity appears to cease in the 10th century, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radhanite.jpg" alt="Radhanites" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Briangotts · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radhanites2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Several etymologies have been suggested for the name. Many scholars, including Barbier de Meynard and Moshe Gil, connect it with a district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> called the land of Radhan in Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> texts of the period. Others derive it from the city of Ray in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, or from the Persian rah, way, and dān, one who knows. Cecil Roth and Claude Cahen suggested a link with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley, Rhodanus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, arguing that the centre of Radhanite activity was probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where all their routes began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ibn Khordadbeh, postmaster and chief of police for the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> under the Abbasid Caliph al-Mu'tamid, is the one source that documents them; Ibn al-Faqih's Book of the Countries of the early 10th century mentions them but draws largely on his work. In his account the merchants carried from the West eunuchs, female slaves, boys, brocade, castor, marten and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, and swords, and brought back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> musk, aloes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a>.</p><p>One route went by sea from Firanja, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, to Farama, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> to al-Kolzum, Suez, and on by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">East Sea</a> to al-Jar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. On the way back some sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and others went to the palace of the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. Another went by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> to al-Obolla and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and China. A land route ran by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Baghdad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, and a fourth through the country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> to Khamlidj, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> capital, across the Jorjan Sea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Muslim and Christian states often banned each other's merchants from their ports, and privateers of both sides raided each other's shipping. The Radhanites acted as neutral go-betweens, and the revenue they brought gave Jewish merchants significant privileges under the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and throughout the Muslim world, which sometimes vexed local Church authorities. Some scholars believe they played a role in the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and helped establish Jewish communities along their routes.</p><p>After their disappearance many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> in regular use in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> vanished from European tables in the 10th century, and the Italian maritime republics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and Amalfi, which had seen them as unwanted competitors, rose. Jewish scrolls of the 11th century found in a cave in Samangan Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, in 2011 have been suggested as a remnant of Radhanites there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Radhanite” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ships from Basra and Siraf sailed to India and China, and a whole section of the market of east Baghdad sold Chinese goods, from silk to porcelain; caravans brought spices, gold and slaves, and Jewish merchants known as the <b>Radhanites</b> linked the Muslim world with Christian Europe.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
